\section*{GPY lower bounds and distances between Gaussian primes}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 028; 2026-09-26), the asserted result is that for each finite step bound, all connected Gaussian-prime components have a common finite size bound, ruling out infinite injective walks, including those meeting axis primes.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 028; 2026-09-26), geometric sampling of a hypothetical long walk produces many residue differences. Shared continuation data create an entropy budget incompatible with avoiding zero residues, yielding a finite periodic sieve barrier and hence the uniform component bound.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The Gaussian-prime component bound gives the negative bounded-step-path answer. The same record also asks substantive GPY prime-gap questions, which that geometric sieve argument does not settle.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 028; 2026-09-26), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: for each finite step bound, all connected Gaussian-prime components have a common finite size bound, ruling out infinite injective walks, including those meeting axis primes.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{GaussianMoat.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.GaussianMoat.fullMain}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

